<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8">
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
    <title>Suez University - Locomotor Module Cartilage Exam (Standard Version with Footer Keys)</title>
    <style>
        :root {
            --primary-color: #0d47a1;
            --secondary-color: #1565c0;
            --bg-color: #f5f7fa;
            --card-bg: #ffffff;
            --text-color: #212121;
            --border-color: #cfd8dc;
        }

        * {
            box-sizing: border-box;
            margin: 0;
            padding: 0;
        }

        body {
            font-family: 'Segoe UI', Tahoma, Geneva, Verdana, sans-serif;
            background-color: var(--bg-color);
            color: var(--text-color);
            line-height: 1.6;
            padding: 20px 0;
        }

        /* Print & Page Layout Configuration */
        .page {
            background-color: var(--card-bg);
            width: 210mm;
            min-height: 297mm;
            margin: 0 auto 30px auto;
            padding: 25mm 20mm 20mm 20mm;
            position: relative;
            box-shadow: 0 4px 15px rgba(0,0,0,0.05);
            display: flex;
            flex-direction: column;
        }

        @media print {
            body {
                background-color: #ffffff;
                padding: 0;
            }
            .page {
                margin: 0;
                box-shadow: none;
                page-break-after: always;
            }
        }

        /* Header Styling */
        .header-table {
            width: 100%;
            border-collapse: collapse;
            margin-bottom: 25px;
            border-bottom: 3px solid var(--primary-color);
        }

        .header-logo-cell {
            width: 15%;
            text-align: left;
            padding-bottom: 10px;
        }

        .header-logo-circle {
            width: 60px;
            height: 60px;
            border: 3px solid var(--primary-color);
            border-radius: 50%;
            display: inline-flex;
            align-items: center;
            justify-content: center;
            font-weight: bold;
            color: var(--primary-color);
            font-size: 1.4rem;
        }

        .header-title-cell {
            width: 70%;
            text-align: center;
            padding-bottom: 10px;
        }

        .header-title-cell h1 {
            font-size: 1.5rem;
            color: var(--primary-color);
            text-transform: uppercase;
            letter-spacing: 0.5px;
            margin-bottom: 4px;
        }

        .header-title-cell p {
            font-size: 0.95rem;
            color: #555;
            font-weight: 500;
        }

        .header-info-cell {
            width: 15%;
            text-align: right;
            font-size: 0.85rem;
            color: #666;
            padding-bottom: 10px;
            font-weight: 500;
        }

        /* Content & Questions */
        .content {
            flex-grow: 1;
        }

        .question-block {
            margin-bottom: 18px;
            page-break-inside: avoid;
        }

        .question-text {
            font-size: 1rem;
            font-weight: 600;
            color: #1a237e;
            margin-bottom: 8px;
            display: flex;
        }

        .question-number {
            margin-right: 8px;
            color: var(--primary-color);
        }

        .options-grid {
            display: grid;
            grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr;
            gap: 8px;
            padding-left: 25px;
        }

        .option {
            padding: 4px 0;
            font-size: 0.9rem;
            display: flex;
            align-items: center;
            position: relative;
            color: var(--text-color);
        }

        .option-letter {
            font-weight: bold;
            color: #555;
            margin-right: 8px;
        }

        /* Footer & Key Banners */
        .footer-banner {
            margin-top: 20px;
            border-top: 1px solid var(--border-color);
            padding-top: 15px;
        }

        .key-banner {
            background-color: #e3f2fd;
            border: 1px dashed var(--secondary-color);
            border-radius: 6px;
            padding: 10px;
            text-align: center;
            font-size: 0.85rem;
            font-weight: bold;
            color: #0d47a1;
            letter-spacing: 0.5px;
            margin-bottom: 12px;
        }

        .footer-info {
            display: flex;
            justify-content: space-between;
            font-size: 0.8rem;
            color: #78909c;
            font-weight: 500;
        }
    </style>
</head>
<body>

    <!-- ================= PAGE 1 ================= -->
    <div class="page">
        <table class="header-table">
            <tr>
                <td class="header-logo-cell"><div class="header-logo-circle">SU</div></td>
                <td class="header-title-cell">
                    <h1>Faculty of Medicine — Suez University</h1>
                    <p>1st Year Medical Students | Locomotor Module: Cartilage Histology Exam</p>
            </tr>
        </table>

        <div class="content">
            <!-- Q1 -->
            <div class="question-block">
                <div class="question-text"><span class="question-number">Q1.</span><span>Which of the following is considered a core physiological criterion of cartilage tissue?</span></div>
                <div class="options-grid">
                    <div class="option"><span class="option-letter">A)</span>It contains a highly dense network of lymphatic vessels.</div>
                    <div class="option"><span class="option-letter">B)</span>It is completely avascular and receives nutrients via diffusion.</div>
                    <div class="option"><span class="option-letter">C)</span>It relies heavily on direct myelinated nerve innervations for mechanical feedback.</div>
                    <div class="option"><span class="option-letter">D)</span>It lacks a regular matrix and is composed entirely of mesenchymal cells.</div>
                </div>
            </div>

            <!-- Q2 -->
            <div class="question-block">
                <div class="question-text"><span class="question-number">Q2.</span><span>At the articular surfaces of joints, where perichondrium is absent, cartilage relies on which source for oxygen and nutrition?</span></div>
                <div class="options-grid">
                    <div class="option"><span class="option-letter">A)</span>Diffusion from the synovial fluid inside the joint cavity.</div>
                    <div class="option"><span class="option-letter">B)</span>Direct capillaries branching from the underlying bone marrow cells.</div>
                    <div class="option"><span class="option-letter">C)</span>Lymphatic drainage via nearby periosteal tissues.</div>
                    <div class="option"><span class="option-letter">D)</span>Aqueous secretions coming directly from chondrocytic vacuoles.</div>
                </div>
            </div>

            <!-- Q3 -->
            <div class="question-block">
                <div class="question-text"><span class="question-number">Q3.</span><span>The perichondrium is structurally designed to cover all surfaces of cartilage EXCEPT at:</span></div>
                <div class="options-grid">
                    <div class="option"><span class="option-letter">A)</span>Costal cartilages and nasal passages.</div>
                    <div class="option"><span class="option-letter">B)</span>The wall of the trachea and major bronchi.</div>
                    <div class="option"><span class="option-letter">C)</span>Articular cartilages of the joints and fibrocartilage.</div>
                    <div class="option"><span class="option-letter">D)</span>The adult ear pinna and eustachian tube.</div>
                </div>
            </div>

            <!-- Q4 -->
            <div class="question-block">
                <div class="question-text"><span class="question-number">Q4.</span><span>What are the primary structural components forming the outer layer of the perichondrium?</span></div>
                <div class="options-grid">
                    <div class="option"><span class="option-letter">A)</span>Chondrogenic cells and fine network of type II collagen fibrils.</div>
                    <div class="option"><span class="option-letter">B)</span>Isogenous clusters of mature chondrocytes and dense elastic fibers.</div>
                    <div class="option"><span class="option-letter">C)</span>Active chondroblasts trapped completely within deep basophilic lacunae.</div>
                    <div class="option"><span class="option-letter">D)</span>Type I collagen fibers, fibroblasts, and functional blood vessels.</div>
                </div>
            </div>

            <!-- Q5 -->
            <div class="question-block">
                <div class="question-text"><span class="question-number">Q5.</span><span>Which cells reside predominantly within the inner cellular (chondrogenic) layer of the perichondrium?</span></div>
                <div class="options-grid">
                    <div class="option"><span class="option-letter">A)</span>Chondrogenic stem cells and immature chondroblasts.</div>
                    <div class="option"><span class="option-letter">B)</span>Mature chondrocytes organized in prominent cell nests.</div>
                    <div class="option"><span class="option-letter">C)</span>Differentiated osteoclasts and inactive fibroblasts.</div>
                    <div class="option"><span class="option-letter">D)</span>Large adipocytes mixed with tight elastic branching fibers.</div>
                </div>
            </div>

            <!-- Q6 -->
            <div class="question-block">
                <div class="question-text"><span class="question-number">Q6.</span><span>Which of the following list of tasks represents a vital function of the perichondrium?</span></div>
                <div class="options-grid">
                    <div class="option"><span class="option-letter">A)</span>Facilitating anaerobic glycolysis inside the central matrix.</div>
                    <div class="option"><span class="option-letter">B)</span>Providing nutrition, muscle attachment, and appositional growth.</div>
                    <div class="option"><span class="option-letter">C)</span>Synthesizing type I collagen to change hyaline into fibrocartilage.</div>
                    <div class="option"><span class="option-letter">D)</span>Preventing the entry of water molecules into the interstitial matrix.</div>
                </div>
            </div>

            <!-- Q7 -->
            <div class="question-block">
                <div class="question-text"><span class="question-number">Q7.</span><span>From which embryonic lineage do the chondrogenic stem cells directly originate?</span></div>
                <div class="options-grid">
                    <div class="option"><span class="option-letter">A)</span>Hematopoietic stem cells within active marrow.</div>
                    <div class="option"><span class="option-letter">B)</span>Epithelial cells lining the early synovial cavity.</div>
                    <div class="option"><span class="option-letter">C)</span>Undifferentiated mesenchymal cells.</div>
                    <div class="option"><span class="option-letter">D)</span>Ectodermal neural crest derivatives.</div>
                </div>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="footer-banner">
            <div class="key-banner">ANSWER KEY (PAGE 1) — Q1: B | Q2: A | Q3: C | Q4: D | Q5: A | Q6: B | Q7: C</div>
            <div class="footer-info"><span>Suez University | Faculty of Medicine</span><span>Page 1 of 6</span></div>
        </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ================= PAGE 2 ================= -->
    <div class="page">
        <table class="header-table">
            <tr>
                <td class="header-logo-cell"><div class="header-logo-circle">SU</div></td>
                <td class="header-title-cell">
                    <h1>Faculty of Medicine — Suez University</h1>
                    <p>1st Year Medical Students | Locomotor Module: Cartilage Histology Exam</p>
            </tr>
        </table>

        <div class="content">
            <!-- Q8 -->
            <div class="question-block">
                <div class="question-text"><span class="question-number">Q8.</span><span>Which of the following descriptions matches the morphology and LM characteristics of a chondrogenic cell?</span></div>
                <div class="options-grid">
                    <div class="option"><span class="option-letter">A)</span>Large spherical cell with multiple peripheral nucleoli.</div>
                    <div class="option"><span class="option-letter">B)</span>Highly vacuolated cuboidal cell filled with dense glycogen deposits.</div>
                    <div class="option"><span class="option-letter">C)</span>Multinucleated giant cell with acidophilic cytoplasm.</div>
                    <div class="option"><span class="option-letter">D)</span>Spindle-shaped cell with an oval, pale basophilic nucleus and light basophilic cytoplasm.</div>
                </div>
            </div>

            <!-- Q9 -->
            <div class="question-block">
                <div class="question-text"><span class="question-number">Q9.</span><span>Under the electron microscope (EM), chondrogenic cells are shown to possess which structural profile?</span></div>
                <div class="options-grid">
                    <div class="option"><span class="option-letter">A)</span>Free ribosomes, few mitochondria, some rER, and a small Golgi apparatus.</div>
                    <div class="option"><span class="option-letter">B)</span>Massive secretory granules filled entirely with type I collagen bundles.</div>
                    <div class="option"><span class="option-letter">C)</span>Extensive parallel arrays of smooth endoplasmic reticulum (sER) only.</div>
                    <div class="option"><span class="option-letter">D)</span>Absence of any organelles except dense clusters of lipid droplets.</div>
                </div>
            </div>

            <!-- Q10 -->
            <div class="question-block">
                <div class="question-text"><span class="question-number">Q10.</span><span>What is the physiological fate and differentiation potential of chondrogenic cells?</span></div>
                <div class="options-grid">
                    <div class="option"><span class="option-letter">A)</span>They divide to form mature fibroblasts in the fibrous perichondrium.</div>
                    <div class="option"><span class="option-letter">B)</span>They undergo division and differentiate into chondroblasts (and osteoprogenitor cells in bone).</div>
                    <div class="option"><span class="option-letter">C)</span>They transform directly into multinucleated chondroclasts during aging.</div>
                    <div class="option"><span class="option-letter">D)</span>They are shed into the synovial fluid to maintain joint lubricity.</div>
                </div>
            </div>

            <!-- Q11 -->
            <div class="question-block">
                <div class="question-text"><span class="question-number">Q11.</span><span>Where are chondroblasts (immature cartilage cells) precisely situated within the cartilage organ layout?</span></div>
                <div class="options-grid">
                    <div class="option"><span class="option-letter">A)</span>Deep within the center of the matrix inside large multi-cellular isogenous groups.</div>
                    <div class="option"><span class="option-letter">B)</span>Exclusively within the outer fibrous layer of the perichondrium.</div>
                    <div class="option"><span class="option-letter">C)</span>At the surface / periphery of the cartilage, forming the chondroblast layer just inside the perichondrium.</div>
                    <div class="option"><span class="option-letter">D)</span>Freely migrating across the territorial matrix near articular spaces.</div>
                </div>
            </div>

            <!-- Q12 -->
            <div class="question-block">
                <div class="question-text"><span class="question-number">Q12.</span><span>The shape of chondroblasts is histologically described as:</span></div>
                <div class="options-grid">
                    <div class="option"><span class="option-letter">A)</span>Irregularly branched with thick cytoplasmic extensions.</div>
                    <div class="option"><span class="option-letter">B)</span>Perfectly spherical with peripheral flattened nuclei.</div>
                    <div class="option"><span class="option-letter">C)</span>Elongated cylindrical structures running perpendicular to the surface.</div>
                    <div class="option"><span class="option-letter">D)</span>Small oval or spindle cells with an elliptic shape whose long axes run parallel to the surface.</div>
                </div>
            </div>

            <!-- Q13 -->
            <div class="question-block">
                <div class="question-text"><span class="question-number">Q13.</span><span>Why does the cytoplasm of chondroblasts appear deep basophilic under the Light Microscope (LM)?</span></div>
                <div class="options-grid">
                    <div class="option"><span class="option-letter">A)</span>Because they are highly active in protein synthesis and possess abundant rough endoplasmic reticulum (rER).</div>
                    <div class="option"><span class="option-letter">B)</span>Due to a high concentration of acidophilic type I collagen fibers.</div>
                    <div class="option"><span class="option-letter">C)</span>Because they store massive amounts of neutral glycogen and clear lipid droplets.</div>
                    <div class="option"><span class="option-letter">D)</span>Due to the complete absence of a nucleolus and a dense euchromatic nucleus.</div>
                </div>
            </div>

            <!-- Q14 -->
            <div class="question-block">
                <div class="question-text"><span class="question-number">Q14.</span><span>An electron micrograph of a cell shows a euchromatic nucleus, rich ribosomes, highly abundant rER, a well-developed Golgi complex, and numerous mitochondria. This cell is most likely a:</span></div>
                <div class="options-grid">
                    <div class="option"><span class="option-letter">A)</span>Fibroblast in an inactive fibrous layer.</div>
                    <div class="option"><span class="option-letter">B)</span>Chondroblast actively synthesizing type II collagen and matrix proteins.</div>
                    <div class="option"><span class="option-letter">C)</span>Chondrocyte residing in an inactive state filled with lipids.</div>
                    <div class="option"><span class="option-letter">D)</span>Mesenchymal stem cell prior to lineage commitment.</div>
                </div>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="footer-banner">
            <div class="key-banner">ANSWER KEY (PAGE 2) — Q8: D | Q9: A | Q10: B | Q11: C | Q12: D | Q13: A | Q14: B</div>
            <div class="footer-info"><span>Suez University | Faculty of Medicine</span><span>Page 2 of 6</span></div>
        </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ================= PAGE 3 ================= -->
    <div class="page">
        <table class="header-table">
            <tr>
                <td class="header-logo-cell"><div class="header-logo-circle">SU</div></td>
                <td class="header-title-cell">
                    <h1>Faculty of Medicine — Suez University</h1>
                    <p>1st Year Medical Students | Locomotor Module: Cartilage Histology Exam</p>
            </tr>
        </table>

        <div class="content">
            <!-- Q15 -->
            <div class="question-block">
                <div class="question-text"><span class="question-number">Q15.</span><span>Chondroblasts are primarily held responsible for which type of cartilage growth?</span></div>
                <div class="options-grid">
                    <div class="option"><span class="option-letter">A)</span>Interstitial growth at the absolute center of the matrix plates.</div>
                    <div class="option"><span class="option-letter">B)</span>Degenerative growth during articular matrix erosion.</div>
                    <div class="option"><span class="option-letter">C)</span>Appositional growth leading to expansion at the periphery of the cartilage.</div>
                    <div class="option"><span class="option-letter">D)</span>Endochondral ossification within the inner fibrous layers.</div>
                </div>
            </div>

            <!-- Q16 -->
            <div class="question-block">
                <div class="question-text"><span class="question-number">Q16.</span><span>Which event directly marks the transition of an immature chondroblast into a mature chondrocyte?</span></div>
                <div class="options-grid">
                    <div class="option"><span class="option-letter">A)</span>The absolute loss of its Golgi apparatus and ribosomes.</div>
                    <div class="option"><span class="option-letter">B)</span>The active synthesis of massive acidophilic type I collagen bundles.</div>
                    <div class="option"><span class="option-letter">C)</span>Migration across the fibrous perichondrium to reach blood vessels.</div>
                    <div class="option"><span class="option-letter">D)</span>When it forms matrix around itself and becomes imprisoned inside a cavity called a lacuna.</div>
                </div>
            </div>

            <!-- Q17 -->
            <div class="question-block">
                <div class="question-text"><span class="question-number">Q17.</span><span>Where are mature chondrocytes located within the structural framework of the cartilage?</span></div>
                <div class="options-grid">
                    <div class="option"><span class="option-letter">A)</span>Deep inside the matrix, trapped within cavities (lacunae) surrounded by condensed matrix.</div>
                    <div class="option"><span class="option-letter">B)</span>Arranged along the outermost borders of the fibrous perichondrium.</div>
                    <div class="option"><span class="option-letter">C)</span>Freely moving inside the synovial cavity of major weight-bearing joints.</div>
                    <div class="option"><span class="option-letter">D)</span>Interspersed evenly between the fibroblastic layers of skeletal muscles.</div>
                </div>
            </div>

            <!-- Q18 -->
            <div class="question-block">
                <div class="question-text"><span class="question-number">Q18.</span><span>What is an "isogenous group" or "cell nest" in cartilage histology?</span></div>
                <div class="options-grid">
                    <div class="option"><span class="option-letter">A)</span>A collection of chondrogenic cells migrating from the blood capillaries.</div>
                    <div class="option"><span class="option-letter">B)</span>A cluster of 2, 4, or 8 chondrocytes originating from division inside the same lacuna.</div>
                    <div class="option"><span class="option-letter">C)</span>A mixture of fibroblasts and chondroblasts within the outer perichondrium.</div>
                    <div class="option"><span class="option-letter">D)</span>A highly condensed region of matrix completely devoid of any cellular components.</div>
                </div>
            </div>

            <!-- Q19 -->
            <div class="question-block">
                <div class="question-text"><span class="question-number">Q19.</span><span>The physiological process where chondrocytes divide and form cell nests inside their lacunae, producing matrix from within, is known as:</span></div>
                <div class="options-grid">
                    <div class="option"><span class="option-letter">A)</span>Appositional growth.</div>
                    <div class="option"><span class="option-letter">B)</span>Asymmetric stem cell division.</div>
                    <div class="option"><span class="option-letter">C)</span>Interstitial growth.</div>
                    <div class="option"><span class="option-letter">D)</span>Metaplastic tissue transformation.</div>
                </div>
            </div>

            <!-- Q20 -->
            <div class="question-block">
                <div class="question-text"><span class="question-number">Q20.</span><span>When comparing the light microscopic (LM) appearance of active vs. less active chondrocytes, a less active (inactive) chondrocyte displays:</span></div>
                <div class="options-grid">
                    <div class="option"><span class="option-letter">A)</span>A deeply basophilic cytoplasm packed with massive new rER.</div>
                    <div class="option"><span class="option-letter">B)</span>Multiple nuclei with highly visible branching mitotic spindles.</div>
                    <div class="option"><span class="option-letter">C)</span>An acidophilic cytoplasm with prominent extracellular type I collagen.</div>
                    <div class="option"><span class="option-letter">D)</span>A highly vacuolated cytoplasm due to being rich in glycogen and lipid droplets.</div>
                </div>
            </div>

            <!-- Q21 -->
            <div class="question-block">
                <div class="question-text"><span class="question-number">Q21.</span><span>What happens as the aggregated chondrocytes inside an isogenous group become highly active in secreting extracellular matrix (ECM) components?</span></div>
                <div class="options-grid">
                    <div class="option"><span class="option-letter">A)</span>The cells are pushed apart and come to occupy separate individual lacunae.</div>
                    <div class="option"><span class="option-letter">B)</span>They immediately fuse together to form a syncytial chondroclastic mass.</div>
                    <div class="option"><span class="option-letter">C)</span>They trigger localized calcification leading to automatic cell death.</div>
                    <div class="option"><span class="option-letter">D)</span>They halt interstitial growth and switch to forming the fibrous perichondrium layer.</div>
                </div>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="footer-banner">
            <div class="key-banner">ANSWER KEY (PAGE 3) — Q15: C | Q16: D | Q17: A | Q18: B | Q19: C | Q20: D | Q21: A</div>
            <div class="footer-info"><span>Suez University | Faculty of Medicine</span><span>Page 3 of 6</span></div>
        </div>
    </div>

