    <div class="page">
        <table class="header-table">
            <tr>
                <td class="header-logo-cell"><div class="header-logo-circle">SU</div></td>
                <td class="header-title-cell">
                    <h1>Faculty of Medicine — Suez University</h1>
                    <p>1st Year Medical Students | Locomotor Module: Cartilage Histology Exam</p>
            </tr>
        </table>

        <div class="content">
            <!-- Q22 -->
            <div class="question-block">
                <div class="question-text"><span class="question-number">Q22.</span><span>What is the primary technical cause behind the irregular shape of chondrocytes and their retraction from the matrix in routine histologic slides?</span></div>
                <div class="options-grid">
                    <div class="option"><span class="option-letter">A)</span>Active phagocytosis by adjacent cells within the cell nest.</div>
                    <div class="option"><span class="option-letter">B)</span>Slight shrinkage of cells and matrix during routine histologic preparation.</div>
                    <div class="option"><span class="option-letter">C)</span>High hydrostatic pressure exerted by the tissue fluid in living states.</div>
                    <div class="option"><span class="option-letter">D)</span>Deposition of specialized type VI collagen network around lacunae.</div>
                </div>
            </div>

            <!-- Q23 -->
            <div class="question-block">
                <div class="question-text"><span class="question-number">Q23.</span><span>In living tissue, what is the true spatial relationship between a chondrocyte and its lacuna?</span></div>
                <div class="options-grid">
                    <div class="option"><span class="option-letter">A)</span>The cell occupies less than 10% of the lacunar volume, floating in fluid.</div>
                    <div class="option"><span class="option-letter">B)</span>The cell forms long branches extending far beyond its own lacuna.</div>
                    <div class="option"><span class="option-letter">C)</span>Chondrocytes fill their lacunae completely.</div>
                    <div class="option"><span class="option-letter">D)</span>The lacuna is structurally absent and only forms during calcification.</div>
                </div>
            </div>

            <!-- Q24 -->
            <div class="question-block">
                <div class="question-text"><span class="question-number">Q24.</span><span>Because the cartilage matrix is completely avascular, how do embedded chondrocytes typically respire and metabolize glucose?</span></div>
                <div class="options-grid">
                    <div class="option"><span class="option-letter">A)</span>Via direct aerobic respiration using oxygen transported by type II fibrils.</div>
                    <div class="option"><span class="option-letter">B)</span>Through specialized oxidative channels connected to the perichondrium.</div>
                    <div class="option"><span class="option-letter">C)</span>By converting glycogen directly into calcium phosphate minerals.</div>
                    <div class="option"><span class="option-letter">D)</span>Mainly by anaerobic glycolysis under low-oxygen tension.</div>
                </div>
            </div>

            <!-- Q25 -->
            <div class="question-block">
                <div class="question-text"><span class="question-number">Q25.</span><span>What structural limit determines the maximum functional thickness of hyaline cartilage in the human body?</span></div>
                <div class="options-grid">
                    <div class="option"><span class="option-letter">A)</span>The maximum limits of passive nutrient and oxygen diffusion through the matrix.</div>
                    <div class="option"><span class="option-letter">B)</span>The total number of available lipid droplets within the cell nests.</div>
                    <div class="option"><span class="option-letter">C)</span>The length of type I collagen bundles running parallel to joint borders.</div>
                    <div class="option"><span class="option-letter">D)</span>The thickness of the outer fibrous layer of the perichondrium.</div>
                </div>
            </div>

